course: dictionary
lesson: 5198
title: Informal words and labels
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words are informal. People use them when talking with friends, not in school reports or letters. Some words are also old-fashioned, so you hear them more in old stories and films than in everyday talk. «A kid sneers at Jamal, "Your dad's a jailbird!"» Sneers shows the kid is being nasty, so the word is being used to hurt.
explain: Jailbird means a person who is in prison, or who has often been in prison. People also use it as a joking or rude label, which is why it can sting. Calling someone a name because of their family is unkind. Minnie does not let the insult win: «My real mum is at home making my tea.» She stays calm and answers with a fact about her own life.
explain: Look at the words around a word to find its feel. Phrases such as «an old jailbird» show it is a label for a person. If you hear a word used to tease someone, it is a rude label, even when its meaning is simple.
fact: Informal words are used in friendly talk, not in formal writing.
fact: Old-fashioned words are used less now and often appear in old stories and films.
fact: «Jailbird» means a person who is in prison or has often been in prison.
fact: «Jailbird» is an informal, joking or rude label, so it can be used to hurt someone.
fact: A label is a name that sticks to a person, and it can be unfair or unkind.
exercise: «A kid sneers at Jamal, "Your dad's a jailbird!"» What does jailbird mean here? | A friend who is always late | A person who keeps pet birds in a cage | *A person who is in prison or has often been in prison | A bird that cannot fly
exercise: What kind of word is jailbird? | A word for a bird's nest | A word that means very happy | A formal word used in school reports | *An informal, old-fashioned word
exercise: «She doesn't even blink: "My real mum is at home making my tea."» What does doesn't even blink suggest? | *She stays calm and unbothered | She is frightened and runs away | She is falling asleep | She is very angry and shouts
test: What does the old word jailbird mean? | *A person who is in prison or has often been in prison | A kind helper at school | A bird that lives in a cage | A tall tower by a river
test: Why is it unkind to call someone a jailbird? | *It is a rude label that can hurt someone | It makes the person happy | It is a name for a kind bird | It is a formal word for a teacher
exam: Is jailbird a word you would use in a school report? | *No, it is informal and old-fashioned, so it suits friendly talk and old stories, not formal writing | No, it is a long word that means very happy | Yes, it is a kind word for a bird | Yes, it is a formal word for a prisoner
